## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count distinct second-shortest paths that share subpaths?  
- How does this adapt if negative weights are allowed but no negative cycles exist?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to blend BFS intuition with shortest-path techniques under a hop constraint and to reconstruct a path from augmented state.

## Key Concepts
- Augmented state graph: (node, hops)
- Dijkstra with 2D dist and predecessor tracking
- Path reconstruction from (node, hops) chain
- Time/space: O((N*H + M*H) log(N*H))

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathWithHops(graph, S, T, H) {
  // graph: {u: [{to,w}, ...], ...}
  const dist = new Map();
  const prev = new Map();
  // min-heap assumed available: push({time, node, hops})
  const heap = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.time-b.time);
  dist.set(`${S}|0`, 0);
  heap.push({time:0, node:S, hops:0});
  while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    const {time,u,k} = heap.pop();
    if (time !== dist.get(`${u}|${k}`)) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of (graph[u] || [])) {
      const nk = k + 1;
      if (nk > H) continue;
      const nd = time + w;
      const key = `${v}|${nk}`;
      if (nd < (dist.get(key) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(key, nd);
        prev.set(key, `${u}|${k}`);
        heap.push({time: nd, node: v, hops: nk});
      }
    }
  }
  let bestTime = Infinity; let bestKey = null;
  for (let k = 0; k <= H; k++) {
    const key = `${T}|${k}`;
    const d = dist.get(key);
    if (d != null && d < bestTime) { bestTime = d; bestKey = key; }
  }
  const path = [];
  if (bestKey) {
    let cur = bestKey;
    while (cur) {
      const [node] = cur.split('|');
      path.push(node);
      cur = prev.get(cur);
    }
    path.reverse();
  }
  return { time: bestTime, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple equal shortest paths?
- How would you optimize memory for very large graphs?
- How would you adapt if hops constraint is on edges vs. nodes?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP understanding with obstacles and modular arithmetic; ensures edge-case handling and ability to implement both bottom-up and top-down approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with blockers (Fibonacci variant)
- Bottom-up vs top-down memoization
- Space optimization (O(1) possible with two variables)
- Modulo handling with BigInt or large integers

## Edge Cases
- **Start blocked (step 0)**: Return 0 immediately — no valid paths exist
- **End blocked (step N)**: dp[N] remains 0 since we cannot land on N
- **Consecutive blocked steps**: Creates a barrier; all steps after a chain of 2+ blocked steps will have dp[i]=0 (cannot be reached)

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1000000007n;

// Bottom-up DP: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysBottomUp(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0n);
  dp[0] = 1n;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) {
      dp[i] = 0n; // blocked step: no ways to land here
      continue;
    }
    dp[i] = (dp[i-1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i-2] : 0n)) % MOD;
  }
  return Number(dp[n]);
}

// Top-down memoization: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysTopDown(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const memo = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1n);

  function solve(i) {
    if (i < 0) return 0n;    if (i === 0) return 1n;
    if (blocked[i]) return 0n; // this step is blocked
    if (memo[i] !== -1n) return memo[i];

    memo[i] = (solve(i-1) + solve(i-2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }

  return Number(solve(n));
}

// Space-optimized: O(N) time, O(1) space
function countWaysOptimized(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let prev2 = 1n, prev1 = blocked[1] ? 0n : 1n;

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0n : (prev1 + prev2) % MOD;
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = curr;
  }

  return Number(n === 0 ? prev2 : prev1);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time**: O(N) for all variants — each step computed once
- **Space**: O(N) for basic DP/memoization, O(1) for optimized version
- **Modulo**: Applied at each addition to prevent overflow

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to blend Trie prefix search with balanced BSTs for dynamic scores, plus a top-k retrieval strategy that scales efficiently. It tests practical update propagation and memory trade-offs in a high-throughput autocomplete system.

## Key Concepts
- Trie for prefix lookup
- AVL/balanced BST per node for phrase-score storage
- Top-k extraction via a front-to-back heap or subtree aggregation
- Update propagation costs and memory considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.words = new AVLTree(); // key: phrase, value: score
    this.topK = new MaxHeap(k); // top-k scores for subtree
  }
}

class AutoCompleteDictionary {
  constructor(k = 10) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.k = k;
  }

  insertPhrase(phrase, score) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of phrase) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.words.insert(phrase, score);
      node.topK.insert(score);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(prefix, k) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTopK(k);
  }
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis
- insertPhrase: O(L log n) where L is phrase length, n is phrases per prefix
- updateScore: O(L log n) for BST updates plus O(L) for heap propagation
- deletePhrase: O(L log n) for BST removal and heap cleanup
- queryTopK: O(L + k log k) for traversal plus heap extraction

## Memory Trade-offs
- Trie structure: O(N × L) where N is total phrases, L is average length
- BST per node: O(N) total across all nodes
- Top-k heaps: O(N × k) worst-case, but typically O(N) with pruning

## Edge Cases
- Duplicate phrases: update existing entry instead of inserting
- Score ties: use lexicographic ordering as tiebreaker
- Empty prefix: return global top-k from root node
- Large k: consider segment tree for better range queries

## Comparison with Alternatives
- Hash map + sorting: O(1) insert but O(n log n) prefix queries
- Suffix tree: better substring search but higher memory overhead
- Pure Trie with scores: O(L) queries but O(n) updates for score changes
- Hybrid approach (proposed): balances prefix efficiency with dynamic updates

